      "claim": "The only sub-20% L2 values are amoxicillin/ampicillin in horses and veal calves (5–12%); these are species-specific and not transferable to the query.",
      "card_ids": ["C16","C18","C26"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral AUC for saxagliptin, an adamantane-containing primary amine, shows substantial oral exposure (~147–153 ng·h/mL after a single oral dose), supporting oral absorption for the adamantane-amine chemotype.",
      "card_ids": ["C41","C49","C50","C56"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 Caco-2 data show small basic amines (norephedrine, cathine, cathinone) are highly permeable (Papp ~8–12 ×10^-6 cm/s), and human jejunal P_eff for leucine is high (~3.6–6.2 ×10^-4 cm/s), supporting intestinal absorption of small ionizable amines; adamantane-containing compounds can also have adequate solubility (OZ277 >2000 µg/mL FaSSGF; AR9281 >100 µM).",
      "card_ids": ["C130","C115","C93","C104","C134","C139","C121","C109"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C130","C115","C93","C104","C134","C139","C121","C109"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct rimantadine-specific absolute oral bioavailability record is supplied.",
    "No matched oral-versus-IV AUC comparison is available for the query molecule.",
    "No first-pass metabolism or clearance data for rimantadine are provided in the cards.",
    "L4 permeability/solubility records are for analogs, not rimantadine itself; Caco-2/jejunal permeability is not a direct F measurement.",
    "Gut-wall efflux and intestinal-metabolism evidence (L5) is not yet available."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F ≥ 20%) with moderate confidence. All transferable human L1 F values are ≥24%; L3 oral AUC for saxagliptin supports adamantane-amine absorption; L4 Caco-2 and human jejunal permeability data for small basic amines support high intestinal absorption, and no affirmative F<20% evidence exists. Uncertainty remains from lack of direct rimantadine F and first-pass metabolism data."
}

